  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThe primary forecast challenges over the next several days are \npotential severe weather, the return of very hot conditions, and \nnear critical fire weather.  \n\nWest CONUS ridge will remain in place through Friday night before \ngradually sliding east over the weekend. Northwest flow aloft \nfrom the ridge will result in multiple shortwaves and WAA that \ncould force occasional thunderstorm activity. An initial \nshortwave could produce a few showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening in the northwest and far southwest. In \naddition, showers and thunderstorms could develop later tonight \nand Friday morning. With limited instability overall, any storms\nthrough the night tonight should remain sub-severe. \n\nAnother round of showers and thunderstorms is then possible Friday \nafternoon into the evening. With much higher instability and around \n40 to 50 kts of deep layer shear, a few of these storms could \nbecome severe. Will continue to message the potential for golf \nball size hail and 70 mph winds. A tornado or two also remains \npossible, though is highly conditional on storm mode and whether\nor not storms can even become surface based to begin with. \nThere remains some uncertainty in the severe threat overall. \nFirst, per the RAP, CIN never fully erodes. This may not \ncompletely inhibit convection. However, it could help result in \nstorms being more elevated, which would inhibit the tornado \nthreat for the forecast area as mentioned. CSU machine learning\ntends to back this scenario with no tornado outline. A second \nbit of uncertainty is smoke. While near surface smoke is more \nlikely behind the surface front (more so a wind shift \nboundary), elevated smoke is likely to remain present across the\nentire forecast area Friday. If this helps limit high \ntemperatures even a degree or two, it could be enough to inhibit\nmore widespread thunderstorm development.\n\nBeyond the severe weather threat Friday, occasional isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms are possible this weekend \nand into the next workweek. However, severe weather \nprobabilities are currently low and mainly for the eastern half \nof the state where higher dewpoints (leading to increased \ninstability) are expected to persist. Currently, none of the \nforecast area is outlined by SPC Saturday or Sunday, although \nCSU maintains marginal equivalent risk probs in the eastern half\nof the state Sunday. Wouldn't be surprised to see the eastern \nCWA clipped Sunday by SPC as well once it enters \"Day 3\" \ntonight.\n\nOtherwise, aforementioned ridge will lead to very hot temperatures \nwith highs in the 100s Friday through this weekend for much of the \nCWA, while the hottest temperatures are expected on Saturday. That \nsaid, heat indices Friday are expected to remain below 100 degrees \nas much drier air will be in place compared to last week, especially \nover the hottest parts of the state (the southwest). Therefore,\nnot issuing a Heat Advisory for Friday at this time. However, \neven hotter temperatures are expected on Saturday and Sunday, \nand will most likely lead to some form of Heat headline when all\nis said and done. One final note is that these hot conditions, \ncombined with breezy winds Friday and Sunday, will lead to near \ncritical fire weather conditions in parts of the southwest.",
